WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:03.570
- [Tutor] So this is a
screenshot of Desmos,

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.200
it's an online graphing calculator,

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.130
what we're gonna do is
use it to understand

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:10.230
how we can go about scaling functions

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:13.360
and I encourage you to go to
Desmos and try it on your own

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.830
either during this video or after.

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:18.290
So let's start with a
nice, interesting function,

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:23.290
let's say f of x is equal
to the absolute value of x,

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:26.024
so that's pretty straightforward.

00:00:26.024 --> 00:00:29.070
Now let's try to create a
scaled version of f of x,

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.680
so we could say g of x is equal to,

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.890
well, I'll start with
just absolute value of x,

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:36.340
so it's the same as f of x,

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:39.017
so we'll just trace the
g of x right on top of f,

00:00:39.017 --> 00:00:41.640
but now let's multiply it by sum constant,

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.940
let's multiply it by two.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:46.324
So notice the difference
between g of x and f of x

00:00:46.324 --> 00:00:49.530
and you can see that g of
x is just two times f of x,

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.080
in fact we can write it this way,

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.505
we can write g of x is
equal to two times f of x,

00:00:54.505 --> 00:00:56.573
we get to the exact same place,

00:00:56.573 --> 00:01:01.573
but you can see that as our x increases,

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.950
g of x increases twice as
fast, at least for positive xs

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:10.300
on the right-hand side and
actually as x decreases,

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.446
g of x also increases twice as fast,

00:01:13.446 --> 00:01:15.564
so is that just a coincidence
that we have a two here

00:01:15.564 --> 00:01:17.787
and it increased twice as fast?

00:01:17.787 --> 00:01:20.120
Well, let's put a three here,

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:24.500
well now it looks like it's
increasing three times as fast

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.758
and it does that in both directions.

00:01:26.758 --> 00:01:31.623
Now what if we were to
put a 0.5 here, 0.5?

00:01:31.623 --> 00:01:35.300
Well now it looks like it's
increasing half as fast

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:39.020
and that makes sense, because
we are just multiplying,

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:42.440
we are scaling how much our f of x is.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:46.610
So before when x equals
one, we got to one,

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.410
but now when x equals one,
we only get to one half,

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:51.690
before when x equals five, we got to five,

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:55.790
now when we get to x equals
five, we only get to 2.5,

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:57.245
so we're increasing half as fast,

00:01:57.245 --> 00:02:00.460
or we have half the slope.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:01.900
Now an interesting question

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.910
to think about is what would happen

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:07.900
if instead of it just being
an absolute value of x,

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:12.010
let's say we were to have
a non-zero y intercept,

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:16.950
so let's say, I don't know, plus six,

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:21.780
so notice then when we change
this constant out front,

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.740
it not only changes the slope,

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:24.870
but it changes the y intercept,

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:28.470
because we're multiplying
this entire expression by 0.5,

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:29.800
so if you multiply it by one,

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:31.615
we're back to where we got before

00:02:31.615 --> 00:02:33.426
and now if we multiply it by two,

00:02:33.426 --> 00:02:35.150
this should increase the y intercept,

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:38.140
'cause remember we're
multiplying both of these terms

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:42.250
by two and we see that, it
not only doubles the slope,

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.720
but it also increases the y intercept.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:46.500
If we go to 0.5,

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:51.000
not only did it decrease the
slope by a factor of one half,

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.130
or I guess you could say
multiple the slope by one half,

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:56.790
but it also made our y intercept

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:59.580
be half of what it was before

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.840
and we can see this more generally

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.300
if we just put a general constant here

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:06.640
and we can add a slider

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:11.640
and actually let me make the
constant go from zero to 10

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.124
with a step of, I don't know, 0.05,

00:03:18.124 --> 00:03:19.760
that's just how much does it increase

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:21.320
every time you change the slider

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:25.010
and notice when we increase our constant,

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:26.420
not only we're getting narrower,

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:29.720
'cause the magnitude of
the slope is being scaled,

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:34.720
but our y intercept increases
and then as k decreases,

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.930
our y intercept is being scaled down

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:41.159
and our slope is being scaled down.

00:03:41.159 --> 00:03:43.590
Now that's one way that
we could go about scaling,

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:45.490
but what if instead of multiplying

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:47.730
our entire function by sum constant,

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:52.600
we instead just replace the
x with a constant times x,

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.625
so instead of k times f of x,

00:03:54.625 --> 00:03:58.892
what if we did it f of k times x?

00:03:58.892 --> 00:04:00.740
Another way to think about it is g of x

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.395
is now equal to the absolute
value of kx plus six,

00:04:03.395 --> 00:04:05.240
what do you think is going to happen?

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.380
Pause this video and think about it.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.450
Well now when we increase k,

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:13.133
notice it has no impact
on our y intercept,

00:04:13.133 --> 00:04:15.557
because it's not scaling the y intercept,

00:04:15.557 --> 00:04:17.780
but it does have an impact on slope,

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:20.750
when k goes from one to two,

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:24.455
once again we are now
increasing twice as fast

00:04:24.455 --> 00:04:28.265
and then when k goes from one to one half,

00:04:28.265 --> 00:04:31.490
we're now increasing half as fast.

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:33.236
Now this is with an
absolute value function,

00:04:33.236 --> 00:04:35.340
what if we did it with a
different type of function,

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.500
let's say we did it with a quadratic?

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:41.180
So two minus x squared,

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.880
let me scroll down a little bit

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:48.170
and so you can see when k
equals one, these are the same

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:50.214
and now if we increase our k,

00:04:50.214 --> 00:04:52.913
let's say we increase our k to two,

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:58.690
notice our parabola is
in this case decreasing

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:00.690
as we get further and further from zero

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:02.066
at a faster and faster rate,

00:05:02.066 --> 00:05:05.502
that's because what you would
have seen at x equals two,

00:05:05.502 --> 00:05:07.620
you're now seeing at x equals one,

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.210
because you are multiplying two times that

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:15.130
and so then if we go between zero and one,

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:18.090
notice on either side of zero,

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:21.396
our parabola is decreasing
at a lower rate,

00:05:21.396 --> 00:05:24.384
it's a changing rate, but
it's a lower changing rate,

00:05:24.384 --> 00:05:26.780
I guess you could put it that way

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:28.560
and we could also try just to see

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:30.640
what happens with our parabola here,

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:34.778
if instead of doing kx, we
once again put the k out front,

00:05:34.778 --> 00:05:36.400
what is that going to do?

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:39.160
And notice that is
changing not only how fast

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:41.173
the curve changes at different points,

00:05:41.173 --> 00:05:43.246
but it's now also
changing the y intercept,

00:05:43.246 --> 00:05:46.290
because we are now
scaling that y intercept.

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:47.280
So I'll leave you there,

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.434
this is just the beginning
of thinking about scaling,

00:05:49.434 --> 00:05:51.970
I really want you to
build an intuitive sense

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:52.827
of what is going on here

00:05:52.827 --> 00:05:55.940
and really think about
mathematically why it makes sense

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:58.600
and go on to Desmos and
play around with it yourself

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:02.153
and also try other types of
functions and see what happens.

